 By Neil Irwin and Courtenay Brown &middot; Nov 25, 2026 
 
 
 Yesterday, we brought you our annual Thanksgiving "economic trends we're thankful for" newsletter. Turns out, this morning's data adds another item to the list: new evidence that we are in a truly exceptional growth boom.

 We unpack it all below. Situational awareness: The White House announced a new round of tariffs on China, the European Union and other economies in retaliation for alleged industrial overcapacity. It's a sign that there will be no course correction on trade policy after Republicans' midterm wipeout. More here . 

 Today's newsletter, edited by Jeffrey Cane and copy edited by Katie Lewis, is 857 words, a 3-minute read. 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: The U.S. economy is ripping 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Wall Street has been reeling amid fears about the future of the AI boom. But in the here and now, the U.S. economy is experiencing an outright boom.

 The big picture: Growth was even stronger this summer than it appeared, and the robust expansion is continuing this fall, according to a range of data released this morning. That is pushing Americans' incomes up and the pace of layoffs down.

 The strong data helps explain why the Federal Reserve is prepared to raise interest rates two weeks from now even in the face of financial market turbulence. Driving the news: The U.S. economy grew at a 4.3% annual rate in the July-through-September quarter, according to the second revision to GDP data — well above the 3.4% originally reported.

 The strong results have continued into Q4. The Commerce Department separately said that personal income rose 0.7% in October, well ahead of analysts' expectations. Overall orders for durable goods came in below forecasts for October, but core capital goods orders — a forward-looking measure of investment spending — climbed 1.2%, also well ahead of projections. The Labor Department said that only 189,000 people filed new claims for jobless benefits last week, matching the lowest of 2026 and among the lowest readings on record (claims go back to 1967). Zoom out: The Q3 GDP reading was powered by business investment, especially for software, showing that the AI buildout remains a powerful driver of overall economic growth.

 It wasn't driven by volatile components of GDP like inventory and trade swings; real final sales to private domestic purchasers surged at a whopping 5.8% pace, and gross domestic income — conceptually similar to GDP — rose at a 4.4% rate. Translation: This isn't some fluky reading, but rather a sign of underlying robustness. Rapid growth looks set to continue in Q4; the Atlanta Fed's GDPNow tool estimates current-quarter growth at 2.9%. Zoom in: Nonresidential fixed investment — the GDP subcategory that covers capital spending — contributed 2.2 percentage points to overall growth in Q3. That was the highest since the bounce back following the 2020 pandemic. Excluding that episode, it was the highest since 2011.

 Yes, but: Personal consumption expenditures rose a moderate 0.3% in October, below analysts' forecasts (+0.4%) and September readings (+0.6%).

 That implies some softness in consumer spending, which, along with AI investment, has been powering the economy forward. The silver lining, however, is that strong income growth paired with modest consumption growth is good news for American household balance sheets: The personal savings rate rose to 4.1%, from 3.6% in September. 
 
