 2. The Fed&#x27;s inflation reality check 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Bureau of Economic Analysis; Chart: Neil Irwin/Axios The strong growth readings affirm the Fed's all-but-certain plans to raise interest rates at a meeting concluding Dec. 9. Inflation data also released in the report shows why.

 By the numbers: The Personal Consumption Expenditures Price Index, which the Fed targets, was up 3.8% over the last 12 months, or 3.3% excluding volatile food and energy.

 In month-on-month terms, both total and core PCE inflation came in at 0.3% for October, implying no meaningful deceleration is underway. The core number annualizes to 3.5% inflation. State of play: While those readings were right in line with forecasts, they underscore that inflation remains far above the Fed's 2% target — as it has been for nearly six consecutive years.

 The combination of rapid growth and elevated inflation looks more like a classic overheating economy — one in which the central bank needs to tighten policy to bring inflation into line. Between the lines: The market turbulence of the last few weeks — the S&amp;P 500 was down 2.8% as of 11:45am ET — points to risks that the AI investment boom may deflate in the quarters ahead.

 But the Fed is unlikely to react to what might happen when growth numbers are coming in so hot and it has been missing its inflation target for so long. What they're saying: "The economy isn't merely hanging in there," Jim Baird, chief investment officer at Plante Moran Financial Advisors, wrote in a note.

 "It continues to demonstrate considerable resilience, with consumers spending, businesses investing, and few signs that restrictive monetary policy is creating significant cracks in the broader expansion," he added. "Boil it all down, and today's report reinforces an increasingly familiar tension: the economy is performing quite well, but its resilience also gives the Fed an even stronger case for further rate hikes." 
 
